-- title:
--   Lebesgue measurability of the open cube
-- statement:
--   For a side length $h > 0$ and a centre $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$, let
--   $$A_h(u) \;=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^d \;:\; \|x-u\|_\infty < h/2 \,\}$$
--   be the open cube of side $h$ centred at $u$, i.e. the open ball of radius $h/2$ for the maximum norm on the coordinates. The claim is that $A_h(u)$ is a measurable set.
--
--   Measurability of the cubes is the entry ticket for every measure-theoretic statement about the tiling of $\mathbb{R}^d$ by cubes: volumes of cubes, integrals of functions that are constant on each cube, and averaging (conditional-expectation) operators built from the tiling all require it.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The set is in fact open, hence Borel, and the positivity hypothesis $h > 0$ is not needed for the conclusion: for $h \le 0$ the defining condition $\|x-u\|_\infty < h/2$ is unsatisfiable and the cube is empty, which is measurable as well. The hypothesis is therefore carried only for uniformity with the surrounding development.
